crypto/tls: send one CertificateRequest certificate type per suite

A TLS 1.2 server sends the certificate_types rsa_sign and ecdsa_sign in
its CertificateRequest, whatever the cipher suite. gemSpec_Krypt
A_22454 requires an eHealth card terminal to send exactly one type:
ecdsa_sign on ECDHE_ECDSA suites, rsa_sign on RSA suites. A Go client
then picks the client certificate that matches the suite.

supported_signature_algorithms lists only the algorithms of that type:
ECDSA and Ed25519 (RFC 8422, Section 5.5) with ecdsa_sign, RSA with
rsa_sign. RFC 5246 doesn't require the two lists to agree, but strict
clients (tlslite-ng) reject a CertificateRequest whose algorithms need a
certificate type it doesn't list, and the server refuses a
CertificateVerify whose algorithm it didn't offer.

This refuses RSA client certificates on ECDSA suites (and the reverse),
which TLS 1.2 allows. It is for the overlay profile of eHealth card
terminals only and is not meant for upstream.

The TLS 1.2 TestClientAuth recordings were regenerated with OpenSSL
1.1.1w (as handshake_test.go requires); the RSA client certificate cases
and TestPKCS1OnlyCert now use a server with an RSA certificate, so that
the suite asks for RSA certificates. The CertificateVerify test of
VerifyUnsupportedSignature uses Ed25519 instead of an RSA scheme, which
the server no longer offers on its ECDSA suite. Five BoGo tests in
which a client signs with an ECDSA or Ed25519 key towards a server with
an RSA certificate are disabled.

ivory: P6, profile kt only. Base: go1.27.2 with P0-P5.
Requirement: A_22454 (gemSpec_Krypt V2.51.0). Upstream: never.
Probe: K3.

